Subject: Re: [seewave] Comparing Audacity's 'Plot Spectrum' and seewave's 'meanspec'
Message-ID: <Tg_nE5_amUE.4hr8kM-qAwAJ@seewave.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <Tg_nE5_amUE.9xNWiTFJBwAJ@seewave.groups.google.com>
References: <Tg_nE5_amUE.9xNWiTFJBwAJ@seewave.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/seewave/c/Tg_nE5_amUE/m/4hr8kM-qAwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Dear Nick,

This is because seewave::meanspec() normalises the frequency spectrum to its maximum so that on a linear scale the maximum is 1, on an uncorrected dB scale the maximum is 0 and on a dB A scale the maximum will depends the frequency of the maximum. The idea behind this was that most of recording systems are not calibrated.
If you wish still to compare spectra you can use the argument norm and turn it to FALSE. The differences seems to be very slight here:

library(tuneR)
library(seewave)
s1 <- readWave("clip_1.wav")
s2 <- readWave("clip_2.wav")
sp1 <- meanspec(s1, norm=FALSE, plot=FALSE)
sp2 <- meanspec(s2, norm=FALSE, plot=FALSE)
plot(sp1[,1], 20*log10(sp1[,2]), type="l", xlab="Frequency (kHz)", ylab="dB")
lines(sp2[,1], 20*log10(sp2[,2]), type="l", col=2)
legend("topright", col=1:2, lty=1, legend=c("clip1", "clip2"))

HTH

Jerome

